window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["gcp-ml"]=[{"id":"gcp-ml-f0001","card":"q-gcp-ml-001-1-setup-prerequisites-and-cost-control","sec":"gcp-ml-s002","q":"What practices keep cost under control when you experiment with ML on Google Cloud?","a":"<p>Use a dedicated project so everything can be removed by deleting the project. Set a billing budget with alert thresholds. Stop notebooks, VMs and endpoints when idle, because they bill while running. Request GPUs or TPUs only when training needs them. Deleting a project is the simplest cleanup because it removes every resource inside it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0002","card":"q-gcp-ml-001-1-setup-prerequisites-and-cost-control","sec":"gcp-ml-s002","q":"A budget in Google Cloud Billing is set to 50 with thresholds at 80% and 100%. Will it stop the spending at 100%?","a":"<p>No. A budget only sends alerts (email, or a Pub/Sub message) when spend crosses a threshold. It does not stop resources or cap spend. To enforce a cap you must react to the Pub/Sub notification, for example with a function that disables billing or stops resources, and that carries its own risk of breaking running services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0003","card":"q-gcp-ml-001-1-setup-prerequisites-and-cost-control","sec":"gcp-ml-s002","q":"Why do you run <code>gcloud services enable aiplatform.googleapis.com</code> before using Vertex AI in a new project?","a":"<p>Google Cloud APIs are disabled by default per project. Until the Vertex AI API (<code>aiplatform.googleapis.com</code>) is enabled, SDK and gcloud calls fail with a \"service not enabled\" or permission error. You can enable several at once by listing them in one command, for example Notebooks and BigQuery APIs too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0004","card":"q-gcp-ml-001-1-setup-prerequisites-and-cost-control","sec":"gcp-ml-s002","q":"Which commands authenticate the gcloud CLI and choose the project it works against?","a":"<p><code>gcloud auth login</code> signs in your user account, and <code>gcloud config set project &lt;project-id&gt;</code> sets the default project for later commands. For code using client libraries, Application Default Credentials (<code>gcloud auth application-default login</code> locally, or an attached service account on GCP) are used instead of the CLI login.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0005","card":"q-gcp-ml-001-1-setup-prerequisites-and-cost-control","sec":"gcp-ml-s002","q":"Which prerequisites does an engineer need before working with Vertex AI, and why GCP core services in particular?","a":"<p>ML basics, Python ML libraries and Jupyter notebooks, plus GCP basics: Cloud Storage buckets (data and model artifacts live there), VMs/compute, and IAM (jobs run under service accounts that need the right roles). Most Vertex AI problems in practice are permission or bucket-location problems, not ML problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0006","card":"q-gcp-ml-002-2-vertex-ai-services-map","sec":"gcp-ml-s003","q":"Give a one-line role for each of these Vertex AI pieces: Studio, Notebooks, Pipelines, Model Registry, Endpoints, Feature Store, Training.","a":"<p>Studio: design and test prompts for generative models. Notebooks: interactive custom model development. Pipelines: orchestrate ML workflows (MLOps). Model Registry: store and version trained models. Endpoints: serve online predictions. Feature Store: manage and serve features. Training: run scalable custom and distributed training jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0007","card":"q-gcp-ml-002-2-vertex-ai-services-map","sec":"gcp-ml-s003","q":"When would you choose BigQuery ML or Vertex AI AutoML instead of writing a custom training job?","a":"<p>BigQuery ML suits analysts who know SQL and have structured data already in BigQuery: you train with <code>CREATE MODEL</code> and avoid moving data. AutoML suits teams without ML expertise who have labeled data and want a model without writing training code. Custom training is for when you need your own architecture, framework, or full control. The trade-off is less control and tuning freedom with the low-code options.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0008","card":"q-gcp-ml-002-2-vertex-ai-services-map","sec":"gcp-ml-s003","q":"Name the end-to-end order in which Vertex AI components are normally used for a custom model.","a":"<p>Prepare features (Feature Store or BigQuery), train (notebook or Training job), save artifacts to Cloud Storage, register the model in Model Registry, deploy it to an Endpoint (or run batch prediction), and automate all of it with Pipelines. Monitoring and evaluation close the loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0009","card":"q-gcp-ml-002-2-vertex-ai-services-map","sec":"gcp-ml-s003","q":"What does this snippet print, and what does it tell you about the Vertex AI SDK?","a":"<p>It prints how many models are registered in that project and region. <code>aiplatform.init</code> sets the default project and region for the SDK, and resources are regional: a model in another location will not appear in this list.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from google.cloud import aiplatform\naiplatform.init(project=&quot;&lt;your-project-id&gt;&quot;, location=&quot;us-central1&quot;)\nmodels = aiplatform.Model.list()\nprint(f&quot;Models in project: {len(models)}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0010","card":"q-gcp-ml-002-2-vertex-ai-services-map","sec":"gcp-ml-s003","q":"Why does <code>aiplatform.init</code> take a <code>location</code> argument, and what goes wrong if you use the wrong region?","a":"<p>Vertex AI resources (models, endpoints, jobs, indexes) are regional. If the location differs from where the resource lives, lists come back empty or calls return \"not found\". Training data buckets should also be in the same region as the job to avoid cross-region latency and egress cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0011","card":"q-gcp-ml-003-model-garden","sec":"gcp-ml-s004","q":"What is Model Garden in Vertex AI, and what kinds of models does it contain?","a":"<p>It is a searchable catalog of models you can filter by modality, task and features. It holds Google foundation models (Gemini), open or third-party models that can be fine-tuned or deployed (for example Llama), and task-specific models for a single job such as object detection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0012","card":"q-gcp-ml-003-model-garden","sec":"gcp-ml-s004","q":"In Model Garden, how do you use a Google foundation model differently from an open model such as Llama?","a":"<p>Google models are used through managed APIs and open directly in Vertex AI Studio, with no infrastructure for you. Open models are deployed to an endpoint you pay for (dedicated compute) or opened in a Colab Enterprise notebook. The trade-off: managed APIs bill per token and need no hosting, while self-deployed open models give weight-level control but cost compute while the endpoint is up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0013","card":"q-gcp-ml-003-model-garden","sec":"gcp-ml-s004","q":"How would you choose between a foundation model, a fine-tunable model and a task-specific solution?","a":"<p>Start with a foundation model and prompting, since it is cheapest to try. Fine-tune when prompting cannot reach the quality, style or domain behavior you need and you have labeled examples. Use a task-specific model when the job is narrow and well defined, such as detection, where a purpose-built model is cheaper and more predictable than a general LLM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0014","card":"q-gcp-ml-003-model-garden","sec":"gcp-ml-s004","q":"In the Vertex AI SDK, what does <code>aiplatform.Model.list()</code> return, and does it list Model Garden foundation models?","a":"<p>It returns models in your own project's Model Registry in the initialized region, such as models you trained or uploaded. It does not enumerate the Model Garden catalog or Google's hosted foundation models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0015","card":"q-gcp-ml-004-generative-ai-models","sec":"gcp-ml-s005","q":"What kinds of tasks do generative text models handle, and why is their output non-deterministic?","a":"<p>Summarization, question answering, classification and sentiment, entity extraction and content creation. The model samples the next token from a probability distribution, so the same prompt can give different answers. Lower temperature makes output more repeatable, but even then exact repeatability is not guaranteed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0016","card":"q-gcp-ml-004-generative-ai-models","sec":"gcp-ml-s005","q":"In the Vertex AI Python SDK, what are the minimal steps to call a Gemini text model?","a":"<p>Install <code>google-cloud-aiplatform</code>, call <code>vertexai.init(project=..., location=...)</code>, create <code>GenerativeModel(\"&lt;model-name&gt;\")</code> from <code>vertexai.generative_models</code>, then call <code>generate_content(prompt)</code> and read <code>response.text</code>. Model names change with releases, so check the current model list (the <code>gemini-pro</code> name used in early examples has since been superseded by newer Gemini versions).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0017","card":"q-gcp-ml-004-generative-ai-models","sec":"gcp-ml-s005","q":"In <code>vertexai.generative_models</code>, what do <code>temperature</code> and <code>max_output_tokens</code> in <code>GenerationConfig</code> control?","a":"<p><code>temperature</code> controls randomness: low values (about 0 to 0.3) give focused, repeatable answers, high values give more varied ones. <code>max_output_tokens</code> caps the response length, which also caps cost, and a response can be cut off mid-sentence if the cap is too low. <code>top_p</code> and <code>top_k</code> also limit which tokens can be sampled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0018","card":"q-gcp-ml-004-generative-ai-models","sec":"gcp-ml-s005","q":"When should you use low versus high temperature for an LLM task?","a":"<p>Low for extraction, classification, code, and factual Q&amp;A where consistency matters. Higher for brainstorming, naming and creative writing where variety is useful. Raising temperature also raises the chance of hallucination.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0019","card":"q-gcp-ml-004-generative-ai-models","sec":"gcp-ml-s005","q":"What is the difference between prompt design, fine-tuning, and distillation for adapting a generative model?","a":"<p>Prompting changes the input only: fast, no training, but limited by context length. Fine-tuning updates model weights (or adapters) with your labeled data to change behavior or style durably. Distillation trains a smaller student model to imitate a larger one, to cut latency and cost. Try prompting first, and tune only when it falls short.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0020","card":"q-gcp-ml-004-generative-ai-models","sec":"gcp-ml-s005","q":"What are the trade-offs of calling a hosted LLM API compared with hosting your own open model?","a":"<p>Hosted API: no infrastructure, pay per token, fast to start, but data goes to a managed service and you depend on the vendor's model lifecycle. Self-hosted: control over weights, data location and tuning, but you pay for always-on GPUs, handle scaling and patching, and get no per-token elasticity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0021","card":"q-gcp-ml-005-prompt-design-in-vertex-ai-studio","sec":"gcp-ml-s006","q":"What is the difference between freeform and structured prompts in Vertex AI Studio?","a":"<p>A freeform prompt is a single open text box. A structured prompt separates context from input/output example pairs, which gives the model few-shot examples of the format you want. Structured prompts give more consistent output for repeatable tasks like extraction or classification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0022","card":"q-gcp-ml-005-prompt-design-in-vertex-ai-studio","sec":"gcp-ml-s006","q":"Why is consistent wording important when you write a prompt that classifies sentiment?","a":"<p>Models are sensitive to wording. If you call the task \"sentiment\" in the instruction but use \"answer\" or \"label\" in the examples, the model sees mixed signals and may format outputs differently. Use the same term for the same thing in the instruction, the examples and the output slot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0023","card":"q-gcp-ml-005-prompt-design-in-vertex-ai-studio","sec":"gcp-ml-s006","q":"How do you make an LLM answer only from supplied text, and what does that reduce?","a":"<p>Put the text in the prompt as context and instruct the model to answer using only that context, ideally also to say it does not know when the answer is missing. This reduces hallucination and is the core of retrieval-augmented generation, where the context comes from a retrieval step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0024","card":"q-gcp-ml-005-prompt-design-in-vertex-ai-studio","sec":"gcp-ml-s006","q":"What does Vertex AI Studio's \"Tune and Distill\" section let you do, compared with just editing the prompt?","a":"<p>It starts a supervised tuning job (or distillation) with your own training data, producing a customized model version. Editing the prompt only changes the input to the base model. Use tuning when prompt changes cannot get consistent domain-specific behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0025","card":"q-gcp-ml-005-prompt-design-in-vertex-ai-studio","sec":"gcp-ml-s006","q":"What practical prompt techniques improve results from Gemini?","a":"<p>Give clear, specific instructions, add few-shot examples, break complex tasks into steps, state the output format (for example JSON or markdown), and supply the context the model needs. Iterate with saved prompts and compare outputs. For repeatable output, lower the temperature.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0026","card":"q-gcp-ml-005-prompt-design-in-vertex-ai-studio","sec":"gcp-ml-s006","q":"Why save prompts in Studio, and what scope do saved prompts have?","a":"<p>Saved prompts let you version and revisit iterations, share them, and export them as code. In Vertex AI Studio, saved prompts are stored per region, so a prompt saved in one region does not show up when you switch to another.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0027","card":"q-gcp-ml-006-colab-enterprise","sec":"gcp-ml-s007","q":"What is the difference between public Colab and Colab Enterprise?","a":"<p>Public Colab is Google-hosted, free or paid per user, and works mostly with data you upload or connect. Colab Enterprise runs inside your Google Cloud project, so it uses your project's IAM, network controls, and data (BigQuery, Cloud Storage) with enterprise security. Use public Colab for quick experiments and Colab Enterprise for governed work on company data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0028","card":"q-gcp-ml-006-colab-enterprise","sec":"gcp-ml-s007","q":"In Colab Enterprise, what do runtime templates do, and why use them?","a":"<p>A runtime template defines the machine type, accelerators, disk, network and idle shutdown for runtimes. Using templates gives a team consistent, pre-approved configurations, and idle shutdown prevents forgotten compute from billing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0029","card":"q-gcp-ml-006-colab-enterprise","sec":"gcp-ml-s007","q":"What would the first cells of a Colab Enterprise notebook typically do to call Gemini on Vertex AI?","a":"<p>Install the SDK (<code>!pip install google-cloud-aiplatform</code>), restart the kernel if needed, authenticate, then run <code>vertexai.init(project=..., location=...)</code> and create a <code>GenerativeModel</code>. In Colab Enterprise the runtime already runs under your project identity, so auth is usually automatic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0030","card":"q-gcp-ml-006-colab-enterprise","sec":"gcp-ml-s007","q":"When would you pick Colab Enterprise over a Workbench notebook instance?","a":"<p>Choose Colab Enterprise for low-ops, collaborative notebook work where Google manages the runtime and you do not need SSH or OS control. Choose a Workbench instance when you need VM access, custom environments or long-running GPU work you configure yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0031","card":"q-gcp-ml-007-gemini-pro-vision-multimodal","sec":"gcp-ml-s008","q":"What does a multimodal Gemini model accept as input and return as output?","a":"<p>It accepts text combined with images or video (newer Gemini versions also accept audio and documents), and returns text. Note that <code>gemini-pro-vision</code> was an early product name; it has been retired in favour of the multimodal Gemini 1.5 and later models, which use the same <code>GenerativeModel</code> API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0032","card":"q-gcp-ml-007-gemini-pro-vision-multimodal","sec":"gcp-ml-s008","q":"In the Vertex AI SDK, what does <code>Part.from_uri(..., mime_type=...)</code> do, and why is the MIME type needed?","a":"<p><code>Part</code> in <code>vertexai.generative_models</code> wraps non-text content. <code>Part.from_uri</code> points to a file in Cloud Storage (<code>gs://...</code>) so the file is not uploaded in the request, and <code>mime_type</code> (such as <code>image/jpeg</code> or <code>video/mp4</code>) tells the model how to decode it. Without the right type the request may be rejected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0033","card":"q-gcp-ml-007-gemini-pro-vision-multimodal","sec":"gcp-ml-s008","q":"Why pass a list to <code>generate_content</code> such as <code>[\"Read the text in this image.\", image]</code> for multimodal calls?","a":"<p>A list lets you send several parts in order (text instructions and media) in one user turn. Order matters for quality: Google's guidance for single-image prompts is to place the image before the text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0034","card":"q-gcp-ml-007-gemini-pro-vision-multimodal","sec":"gcp-ml-s008","q":"What prompting strategies improve multimodal results?","a":"<p>Be specific, give few-shot examples, break complex tasks into steps, state the output format, and for a single image put the image before the text. Few-shot examples help most for custom labels, such as classifying animals into your own categories.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0035","card":"q-gcp-ml-007-gemini-pro-vision-multimodal","sec":"gcp-ml-s008","q":"How are multimodal LLM calls limited and billed?","a":"<p>Every model has a token (context) limit, and images, video and audio consume tokens too. Calls are billed by token or by media unit (for example per image or per second of video, depending on model version), so long videos and many images raise cost quickly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0036","card":"q-gcp-ml-007-gemini-pro-vision-multimodal","sec":"gcp-ml-s008","q":"Where should the image or video live when you call Gemini on Vertex AI, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p>Cloud Storage is preferred for large media: use a <code>gs://</code> URI and make sure the caller's identity has read access to the bucket. For small images you can send bytes inline in the request, which is limited by request size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0037","card":"q-gcp-ml-008-imagen-image-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s009","q":"How does Imagen relate to Gemini, and where do you find it in Vertex AI?","a":"<p>Imagen is a separate family of image models, not part of Gemini. In Vertex AI it appears in Model Garden under vision models and is used for image generation, editing, captioning and visual question answering.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0038","card":"q-gcp-ml-008-imagen-image-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s009","q":"Which Imagen capabilities do <code>ImageTextModel.get_captions</code> and <code>ask_question</code> provide?","a":"<p><code>get_captions</code> returns a short text description of an image, and <code>ask_question</code> answers a free-text question about it (visual Q&amp;A). Both are in <code>vertexai.vision_models</code> and take parameters such as <code>number_of_results</code> and <code>language</code>. Early releases of Imagen had generation and editing limited to approved users; check the current Imagen docs for what is generally available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0039","card":"q-gcp-ml-008-imagen-image-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s009","q":"Why does the Imagen captioning API let you pick a region?","a":"<p>Region selection controls where the data is processed, which matters for data residency and regulatory requirements. Models are also not available in every region, so you pick one where the model exists and that satisfies your compliance rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0040","card":"q-gcp-ml-008-imagen-image-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s009","q":"When would you choose Imagen captioning over sending the image to a multimodal Gemini model?","a":"<p>Imagen captioning is a narrow, cheap, predictable API for short captions or simple visual Q&amp;A. Gemini is a better choice when you need flexible instructions, reasoning across image and text, structured output, or video. The trade-off is flexibility and cost against simplicity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0041","card":"q-gcp-ml-009-speech","sec":"gcp-ml-s010","q":"Which Google models do Vertex AI and Cloud Speech services use for Speech-to-Text and Text-to-Speech?","a":"<p>Chirp is Google's speech recognition model for Speech-to-Text (available through the Speech-to-Text v2 API), and the Text-to-Speech service synthesizes natural voices. Model names evolve, so check the current docs for which voices and models are offered.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0042","card":"q-gcp-ml-009-speech","sec":"gcp-ml-s010","q":"In the Python <code>google-cloud-texttospeech</code> client, what roles do <code>SynthesisInput</code>, <code>VoiceSelectionParams</code> and <code>AudioConfig</code> play?","a":"<p><code>SynthesisInput</code> holds the text (or SSML) to speak. <code>VoiceSelectionParams</code> chooses language code and voice or gender. <code>AudioConfig</code> sets the output encoding, such as MP3 or LINEAR16. They are passed into <code>client.synthesize_speech</code>, which returns <code>audio_content</code> bytes that you write to a file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0043","card":"q-gcp-ml-009-speech","sec":"gcp-ml-s010","q":"In <code>speech.RecognitionConfig</code> for Speech-to-Text, what do <code>encoding</code>, <code>language_code</code> and <code>model</code> do?","a":"<p><code>encoding</code> tells the API how the audio bytes are encoded (for example LINEAR16), <code>language_code</code> selects the language to recognize, and <code>model</code> selects the recognition model (such as a Chirp model, which is served via the v2 API in current releases). A wrong encoding or sample rate commonly gives empty or garbage transcripts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0044","card":"q-gcp-ml-009-speech","sec":"gcp-ml-s010","q":"Short audio can be sent synchronously, but what do you do for long recordings?","a":"<p>Synchronous recognition has short limits (about 1 minute of audio inline). For longer audio use asynchronous (long-running) recognition with audio stored in Cloud Storage, or batch recognition. The quick test panel in Studio also has small limits (about 60 seconds or 10 MB).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0045","card":"q-gcp-ml-009-speech","sec":"gcp-ml-s010","q":"How do you choose between a speech recognition model and an LLM for turning a call recording into a summary?","a":"<p>Use both in sequence: speech-to-text first to get a transcript, then an LLM to summarize or extract fields from the text. Some multimodal Gemini models accept audio directly, but a dedicated recognizer usually gives better transcription accuracy, timestamps and speaker diarization options.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0046","card":"q-gcp-ml-010-5-get-code-and-the-studio-to-notebook-wo","sec":"gcp-ml-s011","q":"What is the \"Get Code\" feature in Vertex AI Studio, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It exports a prompt and its model settings as working code (Python, Node.js, Java, curl) or opens it directly in a Colab Enterprise notebook. It shortens the path from prompt prototype to application, and the settings you tuned (temperature, max tokens, top_p) come with it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0047","card":"q-gcp-ml-010-5-get-code-and-the-studio-to-notebook-wo","sec":"gcp-ml-s011","q":"Describe the workflow from choosing a model to running a prompt in code on Vertex AI.","a":"<p>Find and select a model in Model Garden, open it in Vertex AI Studio, iterate on the prompt and save it, use Get Code to export it, open it in Colab Enterprise, authenticate and install the SDK, then run it and wrap it in application logic. This is fast prototyping before productionizing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0048","card":"q-gcp-ml-010-5-get-code-and-the-studio-to-notebook-wo","sec":"gcp-ml-s011","q":"What is wrong with calling the Gemini REST API with only this header set?","a":"<p>The request has no credential, so Vertex AI rejects it with 401 or 403. Add <code>-H \"Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-access-token)\"</code>, which uses a short-lived OAuth access token. Also note <code>gemini-pro</code> is an early model name, so use a currently supported model.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>curl -X POST \\\n  -H &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot; \\\n  &quot;https://us-central1-aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/&lt;your-project-id&gt;/locations/us-central1/publishers/google/models/gemini-pro:generateContent&quot; \\\n  -d &#x27;{&quot;contents&quot;: [{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;parts&quot;: [{&quot;text&quot;: &quot;Hello&quot;}]}]}&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0049","card":"q-gcp-ml-010-5-get-code-and-the-studio-to-notebook-wo","sec":"gcp-ml-s011","q":"In the Vertex AI <code>generateContent</code> REST URL, what do the project, location and publisher segments mean?","a":"<p>The URL has the form <code>.../projects/&lt;project&gt;/locations/&lt;region&gt;/publishers/google/models/&lt;model&gt;:generateContent</code>. The project is billed and authorizes the call, the location chooses the regional endpoint where it runs, and <code>publishers/google</code> says the model is a Google-published one rather than your own deployed model. The region also appears in the hostname prefix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0050","card":"q-gcp-ml-010-5-get-code-and-the-studio-to-notebook-wo","sec":"gcp-ml-s011","q":"Why prefer the SDK over raw curl when you move from prototype to production on Vertex AI?","a":"<p>The SDK handles authentication through Application Default Credentials, retries, typing and streaming, and it integrates with other Vertex AI services. Curl is useful to debug or to call from languages without an SDK, but you manage tokens and parsing yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0051","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"How does TensorFlow integrate with Vertex AI across the model lifecycle?","a":"<p>Notebook images come with TensorFlow, custom training jobs run TF code on managed compute with CPUs, GPUs or TPUs, distributed training scales across devices, pre-built TF serving containers deploy saved models, and Model Registry and Endpoints store and serve them. Pre-built containers mean you do not write a Dockerfile for standard TF models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0052","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"What is MNIST, and why is it used as a first deep learning exercise?","a":"<p>MNIST is a dataset of 70,000 grayscale images of handwritten digits 0 to 9, each 28 by 28 pixels (60,000 train, 10,000 test). It is small, well understood and trains in seconds, so it is a \"Hello World\" for checking that a pipeline works end to end.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0053","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"In this Keras code, why divide pixel values by 255?","a":"<p>Pixels are integers 0 to 255. Dividing scales them to 0 to 1, which keeps inputs small and similar in scale, so gradient descent trains faster and more stably. The same scaling must be applied at prediction time, or accuracy drops.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>(x_train, y_train), (x_test, y_test) = mnist.load_data()\nx_train, x_test = x_train / 255.0, x_test / 255.0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0054","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"In a Keras MNIST classifier, what are the roles of <code>Flatten(input_shape=(28, 28))</code> and <code>Dense(10, activation='softmax')</code>?","a":"<p><code>Flatten</code> turns each 28 by 28 image into a vector of 784 values so dense layers can use it. The last <code>Dense(10, softmax)</code> outputs a probability for each of the 10 digit classes, and the probabilities sum to 1. The 10 matches the number of classes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0055","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"What does <code>Dropout(0.2)</code> do in a Keras model, and is it active at prediction time?","a":"<p>During training it randomly sets 20% of that layer's inputs to zero on each step, which reduces overfitting by preventing units from relying on each other. It is switched off during evaluation and prediction, where all units are used and activations are scaled appropriately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0056","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"Why does the Keras MNIST model use <code>loss='sparse_categorical_crossentropy'</code> rather than <code>categorical_crossentropy</code>?","a":"<p>Labels are integers (0 to 9). The sparse version accepts integer labels directly. <code>categorical_crossentropy</code> expects one-hot vectors, so you would have to convert labels first. Both compute the same loss.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0057","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"After <code>model.predict</code> on a Keras classifier with a softmax output layer, why take <code>np.argmax</code> of the result?","a":"<p><code>model.predict</code> returns an array of 10 class probabilities per image because of softmax. <code>np.argmax</code> picks the index with the highest probability, which is the predicted digit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0058","card":"q-gcp-ml-011-6-tensorflow-and-mnist-on-vertex-ai","sec":"gcp-ml-s012","q":"How do you move a TensorFlow model trained in a notebook to Vertex AI for serving?","a":"<p>Save the model (SavedModel format), copy the artifact directory to Cloud Storage, then upload it to Model Registry with a serving container image (a pre-built TensorFlow prediction container matching your TF version). Then deploy to an endpoint or run batch prediction. The container version must match the TF version used for training or loading may fail. In newer Keras 3 releases, check how <code>model.save</code> formats differ before exporting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0059","card":"q-gcp-ml-012-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"How do Vertex AI Workbench notebook types differ in control and responsibility: Colab Enterprise, managed notebooks, user-managed notebooks and instances?","a":"<p>Colab Enterprise: lowest control, no VM access, Google patches. Managed notebooks: medium control (PaaS), pre-built containers, no SSH, Google patches. User-managed notebooks: high control (IaaS), SSH and custom OS, you patch. Instances: high control with Google-managed patching, the recommended type. Version note: managed and user-managed notebooks are legacy; Google recommends Workbench instances for new work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0060","card":"q-gcp-ml-012-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"When would you pick a user-managed notebook over a managed one, and what do you take on?","a":"<p>Pick it when you need OS-level control: custom drivers or system packages, SSH access, unusual networking. You take on patching, upgrades and securing the VM. The trade-off is more flexibility but more operational work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0061","card":"q-gcp-ml-012-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"Why might a team choose Colab Enterprise for migrating from public Colab?","a":"<p>Notebooks can be uploaded as they are, the experience is the same, and it runs inside your project with your IAM and data access. It needs almost no setup, so it is a low-friction move when you do not need VM or SSH control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0062","card":"q-gcp-ml-012-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"What can you do with managed notebooks' \"executions and schedules\"?","a":"<p>You can run a notebook as a non-interactive job, for example nightly, saving the output notebook to Cloud Storage, so recurring tasks do not need someone to open a notebook. Version note: this was a managed notebooks feature; for new projects, Workbench instances offer scheduled notebook executions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0063","card":"q-gcp-ml-012-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"Give an interview answer on why Google recommends Workbench instances over the legacy notebook types.","a":"<p>Instances combine the best of both: SSH and VM access like user-managed, plus dynamic hardware changes (stop, resize, attach a GPU), resource monitoring and Google-managed patching like managed. One type replaces the two older ones and reduces the choice you have to make.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0064","card":"q-gcp-ml-013-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"How do you update a Workbench instance to add a GPU, and what must you do first?","a":"<p>Stop the instance, then update machine type and accelerator with <code>gcloud workbench instances update</code> (using flags such as <code>--machine-type</code>, <code>--accelerator-type</code> and <code>--accelerator-core-count</code>), then start it again. The chosen GPU must be available in that zone and compatible with the machine type and image. Check current docs for exact flag support.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0065","card":"q-gcp-ml-013-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"How do you reach the JupyterLab UI and a shell for a notebook VM from the command line?","a":"<p>Describe the instance and read its proxy URI (<code>gcloud workbench instances describe ... --format=\"value(proxyUri)\"</code>) to get the JupyterLab URL, which is protected by Google sign-in. For a shell, use <code>gcloud compute ssh &lt;instance&gt; --zone=&lt;zone&gt;</code> where SSH is allowed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0066","card":"q-gcp-ml-013-comparison","sec":"gcp-ml-s013","q":"Which notebook type gives resource monitoring in the UI but no SSH, and why would you want those meters?","a":"<p>Managed notebooks showed CPU, memory and GPU meters without VM access. The meters let you see whether the GPU is actually busy, so you can right-size the machine or detach the accelerator and avoid paying for idle hardware.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0067","card":"q-gcp-ml-014-gpu-scaling-and-cost","sec":"gcp-ml-s014","q":"In a deep learning notebook, at which stages is the GPU actually used?","a":"<p>During training (the fit loop over epochs), where matrix and linear algebra operations run on the GPU. Data loading, preprocessing, model definition, evaluation and many prediction steps leave it idle, or mostly so. So you can start with a small CPU instance and attach a GPU only for training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0068","card":"q-gcp-ml-014-gpu-scaling-and-cost","sec":"gcp-ml-s014","q":"How do you confirm that TensorFlow is using a GPU?","a":"<p>Call <code>tf.config.list_physical_devices('GPU')</code> and check the list is not empty. TensorFlow places supported operations on a GPU automatically when the drivers and a GPU build are installed, so no code changes are needed. You can also watch GPU utilization in the monitoring meters or with <code>nvidia-smi</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0069","card":"q-gcp-ml-014-gpu-scaling-and-cost","sec":"gcp-ml-s014","q":"How do you right-size GPUs for a training job and keep cost down?","a":"<p>Run one training pass and watch GPU utilization and memory. If it uses only about half of one GPU, more GPUs add cost without speed. Detach or stop the accelerator when you are not training, and use smaller or cheaper GPU types for small models. Stopping a notebook stops compute billing, though disks still cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0070","card":"q-gcp-ml-014-gpu-scaling-and-cost","sec":"gcp-ml-s014","q":"What can cause a GPU to sit at low utilization even during training?","a":"<p>Common causes: the input pipeline cannot feed data fast enough, batch size is too small for the GPU, the model is tiny, or preprocessing runs on CPU. Fixes: larger batches, <code>tf.data</code> with prefetch and parallel reads, mixed precision, and a faster storage path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0071","card":"q-gcp-ml-014-gpu-scaling-and-cost","sec":"gcp-ml-s014","q":"Why must you check GPU type compatibility before scaling a notebook?","a":"<p>Not every GPU type is available in every zone, and not every GPU works with every machine type, driver or base image. Check zone availability (<code>gcloud compute accelerator-types list</code>) and test the setup before committing to a larger configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0072","card":"q-gcp-ml-015-challenge-set-up-a-notebook-for-the-expe","sec":"gcp-ml-s015","q":"How would you bring a notebook from a public tutorial page into a Workbench instance?","a":"<p>Open it in public Colab or from its repository, download it as an <code>.ipynb</code> file, upload it into JupyterLab on the instance, then pick the TensorFlow kernel. Check that the TF version matches the notebook's requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0073","card":"q-gcp-ml-015-challenge-set-up-a-notebook-for-the-expe","sec":"gcp-ml-s015","q":"In this Keras CNN, why is the extra reshape needed before training?","a":"<p><code>Conv2D</code> layers expect 4D input of (batch, height, width, channels). MNIST images are 28 by 28 with no channel axis, so the reshape adds a channel dimension of 1 for grayscale. <code>-1</code> lets NumPy infer the batch size. The <code>astype('float32')</code> and division by 255 normalize pixel values.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x_train = x_train.reshape(-1, 28, 28, 1).astype(&#x27;float32&#x27;) / 255.0\nx_test = x_test.reshape(-1, 28, 28, 1).astype(&#x27;float32&#x27;) / 255.0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0074","card":"q-gcp-ml-015-challenge-set-up-a-notebook-for-the-expe","sec":"gcp-ml-s015","q":"What do <code>Conv2D</code> followed by <code>MaxPooling2D</code> do in a CNN?","a":"<p><code>Conv2D</code> slides small filters (here 3 by 3) over the image to detect local patterns such as edges, producing feature maps. <code>MaxPooling2D</code> downsamples each map by keeping the maximum in each window, which reduces size and compute and gives some translation tolerance. Stacking them builds up from simple to complex patterns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0075","card":"q-gcp-ml-015-challenge-set-up-a-notebook-for-the-expe","sec":"gcp-ml-s015","q":"In <code>model.fit(x_train, y_train, epochs=5, batch_size=64, validation_split=0.1)</code>, what do <code>batch_size</code> and <code>validation_split</code> do?","a":"<p><code>batch_size</code> is the number of samples per gradient update, which trades memory use and speed against gradient noise. <code>validation_split=0.1</code> holds out the last 10% of the training data and reports loss and accuracy on it after each epoch, which shows overfitting. The test set stays untouched until the final <code>evaluate</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0076","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"What is an embedding, and why does vector search find results that keyword search misses?","a":"<p>An embedding is a numeric vector produced by a model that captures meaning, so texts (or images or audio) with similar meaning lie close together. Vector search compares these vectors, so \"install GPU drivers\" can match \"set up CUDA\" even without shared words. Keyword search only matches terms, so it misses paraphrases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0077","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"Why use Approximate Nearest Neighbor search instead of exact search?","a":"<p>Exact search compares the query with every vector, which is too slow at millions or billions of items. ANN builds an index structure so it checks only promising candidates, giving large speedups with a small, tunable loss in recall. The trade-off is that you may miss a true nearest neighbor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0078","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"What is a similarity metric in vector search, and which are common?","a":"<p>It is the function that scores how close two vectors are. Common ones: cosine similarity (angle, ignores magnitude), dot product (angle and magnitude), and Euclidean (L2) distance. Use the metric the embedding model was trained for. For normalized embeddings, cosine and dot product give the same ranking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0079","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"How does vector search support RAG?","a":"<p>Documents are embedded and indexed ahead of time. At question time you embed the query, retrieve the nearest chunks, and insert them into the LLM prompt as context. This grounds answers in your data, reduces hallucination, and lets you update knowledge by re-indexing rather than retraining the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0080","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"What are the steps to build a semantic search service on Vertex AI Vector Search?","a":"<p>Prepare and chunk the data, create embeddings with an embedding model, store them in Cloud Storage in the supported format, create an ANN index, create an index endpoint, deploy the index to the endpoint, then embed each query and call the endpoint to find neighbors. The deploy step is slow and the index endpoint bills while deployed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0081","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"Why must the index <code>dimensions</code> setting match the embedding model's output?","a":"<p>The index stores fixed-length vectors. If <code>dimensions</code> differs from the vector length (for example 768 for the older <code>textembedding-gecko</code> model), loading fails or results are meaningless. The same embedding model must be used for documents and queries, or distances are not comparable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0082","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"What are tree-AH and brute-force index types in Vertex AI Vector Search?","a":"<p>Tree-AH is the approximate algorithm: a tree partitions the space and asymmetric hashing (quantization) scores candidates fast, so it scales to large data. Brute force compares every vector exactly and is useful to measure the recall of an approximate index or for small datasets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0083","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"When would you choose Vertex AI Vector Search over ChromaDB or Pinecone?","a":"<p>Choose Vertex AI Vector Search when your stack is GCP-native (BigQuery, Cloud Storage, Vertex AI), you want managed scaling and IAM, and low latency at large scale. Choose ChromaDB for local development or small projects, and Pinecone or others for a multi-cloud SaaS option. The trade-off is lock-in and always-on deployment cost versus portability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0084","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"Version note: what happened to the names \"Matching Engine\" and <code>MatchingEngineIndex</code> in the Vertex AI SDK?","a":"<p>Matching Engine was the earlier name of the service now called Vertex AI Vector Search. The SDK classes kept the old names for a long time (<code>MatchingEngineIndex</code>, <code>MatchingEngineIndexEndpoint</code>), and newer releases have added a Vector Search naming, so check the SDK version you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0085","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"In the Python SDK, what is the purpose of <code>index_endpoint.deploy_index(index=..., deployed_index_id=...)</code>?","a":"<p>Creating an index only stores it. <code>deploy_index</code> loads the index onto serving nodes behind an index endpoint so it can answer queries. <code>deployed_index_id</code> is the identifier you pass when you query that deployment. You can deploy several indexes to one endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0086","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"When creating embeddings for a large BigQuery table, why read and embed the rows in chunks?","a":"<p>A large table may not fit in memory, and embedding APIs have per-request limits on batch size and tokens. Processing in chunks keeps memory bounded, respects rate limits, and lets you retry a failed chunk. Combining title and body into one text gives each embedding richer context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0087","card":"q-gcp-ml-016-8-vector-search-embeddings-ann-rag","sec":"gcp-ml-s016","q":"How can you verify that an index was built correctly after deployment?","a":"<p>Compare the index's vector count with the number of embeddings you uploaded, check dimensions and algorithm in the console, and run test queries whose true neighbors you know. A mismatch in count often points to malformed records skipped during ingestion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0088","card":"q-gcp-ml-017-stack-overflow-example-workflow","sec":"gcp-ml-s017","q":"In what order do you run these RAG indexing steps, and why: embed data, create ANN index, create endpoint, deploy index, query?","a":"<p>Embeddings must exist first, because the index is built from them. The index must exist before it can be deployed. An endpoint is needed as the serving host, then you deploy the index onto it, and only then can you query. Each step consumes the output of the previous one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0089","card":"q-gcp-ml-017-stack-overflow-example-workflow","sec":"gcp-ml-s017","q":"Why must queries use the same embedding model as the indexed documents?","a":"<p>Different models map text into different vector spaces, so distances between a query vector from one model and document vectors from another carry no meaning. Using the same model (and the same task type or settings) keeps both in one space.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0090","card":"q-gcp-ml-017-stack-overflow-example-workflow","sec":"gcp-ml-s017","q":"What do <code>approximate_neighbors_count</code> and <code>num_neighbors</code> mean in Vertex AI Vector Search?","a":"<p><code>approximate_neighbors_count</code> (index creation) is the default number of neighbors the index should return and shapes the search. <code>num_neighbors</code> (query time) is how many results you ask <code>find_neighbors</code> for. Larger values give better recall but raise latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0091","card":"q-gcp-ml-017-stack-overflow-example-workflow","sec":"gcp-ml-s017","q":"If you embed text for a question-and-answer retrieval system, what extra detail do newer embedding models ask for?","a":"<p>Newer Vertex AI embedding models take a <code>task_type</code> (for example retrieval query versus retrieval document) so the same model embeds queries and documents appropriately. Older models such as <code>textembedding-gecko</code> did not require this; they have since been deprecated in favor of newer embedding models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0092","card":"q-gcp-ml-017-stack-overflow-example-workflow","sec":"gcp-ml-s017","q":"Why is index creation and deployment slow, and how do you manage its cost?","a":"<p>Building an ANN index over large embedding sets and provisioning serving nodes takes a long time (minutes to hours). Deployed indexes bill for their nodes while deployed, so undeploy and delete test indexes when finished.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0093","card":"q-gcp-ml-018-9-feature-store","sec":"gcp-ml-s018","q":"What problem does a feature store solve?","a":"<p>It gives one governed place to define, share and serve features, so training and serving use the same definitions. This removes duplicated feature code between teams and reduces training-serving skew, where a model sees features computed differently in production than in training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0094","card":"q-gcp-ml-018-9-feature-store","sec":"gcp-ml-s018","q":"What is the difference between online and offline feature serving?","a":"<p>Online serving returns the latest feature values for a few entities with low latency, for real-time predictions. Offline serving returns large sets of historical values, usually point-in-time correct, for training or batch prediction. Vertex AI Feature Store serves offline data from BigQuery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0095","card":"q-gcp-ml-018-9-feature-store","sec":"gcp-ml-s018","q":"How does Vertex AI Feature Store use BigQuery, and why is that useful?","a":"<p>The newer Vertex AI Feature Store acts as a metadata and serving layer on top of BigQuery tables or views, so features are not copied into a separate offline store. That reduces data duplication, keeps existing governance and lets you publish features from existing pipelines. Version note: the older Feature Store (with <code>Featurestore</code>, entity types and fixed online nodes) is the legacy design; newer projects use the BigQuery-based version with feature groups and online stores.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0096","card":"q-gcp-ml-018-9-feature-store","sec":"gcp-ml-s018","q":"When would you use a feature store instead of a database or a Cloud Storage bucket for features?","a":"<p>Use it when several models share features, you need both low-latency serving and batch training reads, and you want versioning, metadata and monitoring. A bucket is fine for plain batch files but is not designed for low-latency online reads. A database can serve online reads but you build governance, versioning and offline access yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0097","card":"q-gcp-ml-018-9-feature-store","sec":"gcp-ml-s018","q":"What is training-serving skew, and how does a feature store reduce it?","a":"<p>Training-serving skew is a difference between feature values or computation at training time and at serving time, which degrades accuracy silently. A feature store defines each feature once and serves it for both purposes, so the same logic and data feed both paths.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0098","card":"q-gcp-ml-018-9-feature-store","sec":"gcp-ml-s018","q":"What is feature monitoring or drift detection, and why use it?","a":"<p>It compares feature value distributions over time (or against a training baseline) and alerts on shifts. Drift in inputs often precedes a drop in model quality, so the alert tells you to investigate or retrain before users notice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0099","card":"q-gcp-ml-018-9-feature-store","sec":"gcp-ml-s018","q":"What steps are needed to make features available for serving in Vertex AI Feature Store?","a":"<p>Prepare the feature data (for example in BigQuery), create the store or online store pointing at that source, define the features with names, types and metadata, then serve them online or offline. Streaming ingestion can update online values in near real time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0100","card":"q-gcp-ml-019-challenge-solution-feature-store-with-th","sec":"gcp-ml-s019","q":"In the legacy Vertex AI Feature Store (<code>aiplatform.Featurestore</code>), what is an entity type and how does it relate to features?","a":"<p>An entity type groups semantically related features about one kind of object, such as users or products. Each feature belongs to an entity type, and each row of values is keyed by an entity ID. Version note: this is the legacy model; the newer BigQuery-based version uses feature groups and feature views instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0101","card":"q-gcp-ml-019-challenge-solution-feature-store-with-th","sec":"gcp-ml-s019","q":"In the legacy Feature Store SDK, what does <code>online_store_fixed_node_count</code> set, and what is the cost implication?","a":"<p>It sets how many nodes serve the online store. Nodes bill continuously while the store exists, even when idle, so a test store left running costs money. Delete test stores when finished. Version note: legacy Feature Store API; check current docs for the newer service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0102","card":"q-gcp-ml-019-challenge-solution-feature-store-with-th","sec":"gcp-ml-s019","q":"Why does ingesting a pandas DataFrame into a legacy Feature Store require an entity ID column and a timestamp?","a":"<p>The entity ID says which entity each row belongs to, so values can be looked up by key. The timestamp (<code>feature_time</code>) records when the value was valid, which allows point-in-time queries and avoids leaking future values into training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0103","card":"q-gcp-ml-019-challenge-solution-feature-store-with-th","sec":"gcp-ml-s019","q":"Why drop or impute null values before ingesting a pandas DataFrame into Vertex AI Feature Store?","a":"<p>Missing values can fail type checks at ingestion or carry through to training and serving as errors. Cleaning (or imputing) nulls first keeps feature values consistent. If nulls are meaningful, handle them explicitly rather than dropping rows blindly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0104","card":"q-gcp-ml-019-challenge-solution-feature-store-with-th","sec":"gcp-ml-s019","q":"What does <code>entity_type.read(entity_ids=[...])</code> do in the legacy Feature Store SDK?","a":"<p>It performs an online read: it fetches the latest feature values for the listed entity IDs from the online store with low latency. It is a quick way to verify that ingestion worked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0105","card":"q-gcp-ml-019-challenge-solution-feature-store-with-th","sec":"gcp-ml-s019","q":"Why does creating a legacy Feature Store take a long time, and what does that mean for a pipeline?","a":"<p>Provisioning serving nodes takes many minutes (around 15 in practice). Create the store once, outside any frequently running job, and reuse it, rather than creating it inside each run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0106","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"What is Vertex AI Model Registry, and which problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It is a central repository for the lifecycle of your models: custom, AutoML and BigQuery ML models in one place, with versions, aliases, labels, lineage and one-click deployment. It solves scattered artifacts, unclear which model is in production, and no history of what changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0107","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"What is the difference between a model version, an alias and a label in Model Registry?","a":"<p>A version is an immutable numbered snapshot of a model. An alias is a movable name like <code>stable</code> or <code>champion</code> that points to one version, so deployments can reference the alias instead of a number. A label is a key-value tag for filtering and organization, not tied to a version number.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0108","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"Why would a deployment reference a model alias rather than a version number?","a":"<p>The alias can be moved to a new version without changing deployment code or configuration, which makes promotion and rollback a metadata change. A fixed version number gives exact reproducibility but needs a config change each release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0109","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"Which kinds of models can be registered in Vertex AI Model Registry?","a":"<p>Custom models (TensorFlow, PyTorch, scikit-learn and others), AutoML models (text, tabular, image, video) and BigQuery ML models trained with SQL. Having them in one registry lets you deploy and track them the same way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0110","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"What is the typical lifecycle of a model in the registry?","a":"<p>Import or train the model, register it as version 1, add aliases and labels, evaluate it, deploy it to an endpoint (or run batch prediction), then upload later versions of the same model and promote by changing the default version or alias. Each version keeps its artifacts and container image for lineage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0111","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"In <code>aiplatform.Model.upload</code>, what are <code>artifact_uri</code> and <code>serving_container_image_uri</code> for?","a":"<p><code>artifact_uri</code> is the Cloud Storage folder holding the saved model files. <code>serving_container_image_uri</code> is the container image that loads those files and serves predictions (a pre-built framework image or your custom one). Both are required for a model to be deployable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0112","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"Why must the serving container version match the framework used to save the model?","a":"<p>The container loads the artifact with a specific runtime. A model saved with one TensorFlow version may fail or behave differently in a container for another version. Match the pre-built container (for example <code>tf2-cpu.2-12</code>) to the training version, or use a custom container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0113","card":"q-gcp-ml-020-10-model-registry","sec":"gcp-ml-s020","q":"What is the registry's default version, and what does <code>is_default_version</code> change?","a":"<p>The default version is what a request for the model resolves to when no version is named. Setting <code>is_default_version=True</code> on upload makes that version the default, and <code>False</code> keeps the existing default so a new version can be tested before promotion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0114","card":"q-gcp-ml-021-register-two-versions-of-a-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s021","q":"In <code>aiplatform.Model.upload</code>, how do you upload a second version to an existing model instead of creating a new model?","a":"<p>Pass <code>parent_model</code> with the first model's resource name and keep the same <code>display_name</code>. Vertex AI then adds a new version to the same registry model, instead of creating a separate model. Without <code>parent_model</code>, you get a second unrelated model with the same name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0115","card":"q-gcp-ml-021-register-two-versions-of-a-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s021","q":"Why upload the new version with <code>is_default_version=False</code> and aliases such as <code>experimental</code>?","a":"<p>This lets you register the new version and test it without changing what the default or <code>stable</code> alias serves. After validation you promote it by moving the alias or making it default, and roll back by moving the alias back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0116","card":"q-gcp-ml-021-register-two-versions-of-a-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s021","q":"Where must model files be before <code>Model.upload</code> is called, and why?","a":"<p>In Cloud Storage. Vertex AI reads <code>artifact_uri</code> from a <code>gs://</code> path at deployment time, so a local directory does not work. Save the model locally, copy it with <code>gsutil cp -r</code> or <code>gcloud storage cp -r</code>, then upload.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0117","card":"q-gcp-ml-021-register-two-versions-of-a-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s021","q":"How would you list all versions of a registered model in the SDK?","a":"<p>Use <code>aiplatform.Model.list</code> with a filter on <code>display_name</code>, or use the model registry's versioning API (<code>model_registry.list_versions()</code>) to see each version ID and aliases. The <code>version_id</code> and <code>version_aliases</code> properties show each entry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0118","card":"q-gcp-ml-021-register-two-versions-of-a-model","sec":"gcp-ml-s021","q":"When would you register two versions of a model, and what would you compare before promoting?","a":"<p>Register a new version after retraining or changing the architecture. Compare evaluation metrics on the same held-out data, latency and size, then roll out gradually (for example by splitting endpoint traffic) before making it default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0119","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"What is the difference between online and batch prediction in Vertex AI, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Online prediction is synchronous: you deploy a model to an endpoint and get answers in milliseconds to seconds, for web or mobile apps. Batch prediction is an asynchronous job that reads a large input set from Cloud Storage or BigQuery and writes results to storage. Use online for low-latency individual requests and batch for large, non-urgent jobs, which are cheaper because compute exists only during the job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0120","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"Does batch prediction need an endpoint?","a":"<p>No. You submit a batch prediction job directly from the model (<code>model.batch_predict</code>), and Vertex AI provisions compute, runs the job and releases the compute. Only online prediction needs a deployed endpoint, which bills while it is up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0121","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"What does a Vertex AI endpoint provide that you would otherwise build yourself?","a":"<p>It wraps your model in managed serving: containers on VMs, load balancing, autoscaling, health checks, logging and monitoring, plus the HTTPS API. You do not have to run Kubernetes or Cloud Run yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0122","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"What do <code>min_replica_count</code> and <code>max_replica_count</code> do in <code>model.deploy</code>?","a":"<p>They set the autoscaling range for the deployed model's replicas. <code>min_replica_count</code> is always-on capacity (and a continuous cost, with 1 or more avoiding cold starts). <code>max_replica_count</code> caps scaling under load, which bounds both cost and capacity. Autoscaling responds to CPU or accelerator utilization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0123","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"What happens to cost if you leave a model deployed on an endpoint with <code>min_replica_count=1</code> and no traffic?","a":"<p>You still pay for the node hours, because deployed replicas run continuously. Undeploy models you are not using, or use batch prediction for occasional jobs. Always undeploy before deleting an endpoint, or use <code>undeploy_all</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0124","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"Why must you undeploy models from an endpoint before deleting it?","a":"<p>Deployed models are attached to the endpoint and consume compute. An endpoint with deployed models cannot be deleted until they are undeployed (the SDK's <code>endpoint.delete(force=True)</code> can undeploy for you). This is also how you stop the node-hour billing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0125","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"How can you deploy two model versions behind one endpoint and shift traffic between them?","a":"<p>Deploy both to the same endpoint and set <code>traffic_split</code> percentages (for example 90 and 10) on deploy. This enables canary releases and A/B tests, and you can move traffic to 100% on the new version or back to the old one without client changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0126","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"What does this online prediction call send and return?","a":"<p>It sends a list of input instances (a JSON-serializable list, here from NumPy <code>.tolist()</code>) to the endpoint, and returns a response whose <code>predictions</code> list has one result per instance. For TensorFlow classification this is typically the class probabilities.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>prediction = endpoint.predict(instances=test_input)\nprint(prediction.predictions[0][:5])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0127","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"In <code>model.batch_predict</code>, what do <code>instances_format</code>, <code>gcs_source</code> and <code>gcs_destination_prefix</code> configure?","a":"<p><code>instances_format</code> tells Vertex AI how the input is encoded (such as <code>jsonl</code>, <code>csv</code> or BigQuery). <code>gcs_source</code> is the input location, and <code>gcs_destination_prefix</code> is the folder where prediction output files are written. <code>batch_job.wait()</code> blocks until the job finishes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0128","card":"q-gcp-ml-022-11-endpoints-online-vs-batch-prediction","sec":"gcp-ml-s022","q":"What is the end-to-end deployment flow from trained model to predictions?","a":"<p>Train, save artifacts, copy them to Cloud Storage, upload to Model Registry with a serving container, manage versions, then choose a path: create an endpoint and deploy for online prediction, or submit a batch job from the model for bulk prediction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0129","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"What is Vertex AI Pipelines and why use it over running notebooks by hand?","a":"<p>It is a serverless service that runs ML workflows defined as pipelines (KFP or TFX) with managed infrastructure. Pipelines give repeatability, scheduled and triggered runs, tracked artifacts and lineage, caching, and a visual graph. A manual notebook is not reproducible or easy to audit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0130","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"Which frameworks can you use to define Vertex AI Pipelines?","a":"<p>Kubeflow Pipelines (KFP) SDK and TensorFlow Extended (TFX). KFP is the common choice, and the same compiled pipeline definition runs on Vertex AI's managed backend.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0131","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"What is a DAG in an ML pipeline, and how is it defined?","a":"<p>A directed acyclic graph where nodes are steps (components) and edges are data dependencies, with no cycles. In KFP the graph comes from how you connect component outputs to inputs, for example passing <code>train_task.output</code> into the next step, and independent steps can run in parallel.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0132","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"In KFP, what do the <code>@component</code> and <code>@pipeline</code> decorators do?","a":"<p><code>@component</code> turns a Python function into a pipeline step that runs in its own container, with typed inputs and outputs. <code>@pipeline</code> defines how those steps are wired into a graph. The pipeline function describes the graph at compile time; it is not executed as normal Python at run time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0133","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"What is wrong or risky in this component?","a":"<p>By default a KFP lightweight component runs in a base Python image that does not include TensorFlow, so the <code>import</code> fails unless you set <code>packages_to_install=[\"tensorflow\"]</code> or a <code>base_image</code> with it. Imports must be inside the function because only that function's source is packaged into the container.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@component\ndef train_model(dataset_uri: str) -&gt; str:\n    import tensorflow as tf\n    ...\n    return model_path</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0134","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"What does compiling a pipeline produce, and why is compiling a separate step from running it?","a":"<p><code>compiler.Compiler().compile</code> writes a pipeline definition file (JSON or YAML) describing steps, containers and wiring. Compiling separately lets you version the definition in source control, review it, and run the same artifact repeatedly, from any environment or schedule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0135","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"In <code>aiplatform.PipelineJob</code>, what are <code>template_path</code>, <code>parameter_values</code> and <code>pipeline_root</code> for?","a":"<p><code>template_path</code> is the compiled pipeline file. <code>parameter_values</code> are the run-time inputs to the pipeline function. <code>pipeline_root</code> is a Cloud Storage location where Vertex AI stores artifacts and metadata for the run. Missing or inaccessible <code>pipeline_root</code> is a common failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0136","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"What is the difference between <code>job.run(sync=True)</code> and <code>job.run(sync=False)</code>?","a":"<p><code>sync=True</code> blocks until the pipeline finishes, which suits scripts that must continue after completion. <code>sync=False</code> returns immediately after submission, so you poll status or watch in the console. Use non-blocking for long runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0137","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"How do you pass data between KFP components, and what is the difference between small values and artifacts?","a":"<p>Pass a component's output into the next component's input, and KFP wires the dependency. Small values such as strings or numbers are passed as parameters. Large data, such as datasets and models, should be passed as artifacts (typed <code>Input</code>/<code>Output</code> paths in Cloud Storage), not as Python values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0138","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"What is the Template Gallery in Vertex AI Pipelines?","a":"<p>A set of ready-made pipeline templates for common workflows, which you can run from the UI or download and customize. They help you start from a tested structure, and you can also upload your own templates for team reuse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0139","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"How does Vertex AI Pipelines connect with the rest of Vertex AI?","a":"<p>A pipeline can read features from Feature Store or BigQuery, run training jobs, evaluate the model, register it in Model Registry, deploy it to an Endpoint and trigger monitoring. Each step records artifacts and lineage in Vertex ML Metadata.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0140","card":"q-gcp-ml-023-12-vertex-ai-pipelines-mlops","sec":"gcp-ml-s023","q":"Why are pipelines important for MLOps?","a":"<p>They automate retraining and deployment, make runs reproducible, record lineage for audits, and allow scheduled or event-triggered runs. They also enforce evaluation gates, for example only registering a model whose metric passes a threshold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0141","card":"q-gcp-ml-024-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"Why use Google Cloud Pipeline Components such as <code>CustomTrainingJobOp</code> and <code>ModelUploadOp</code> instead of writing your own components?","a":"<p>They are maintained wrappers around Vertex AI operations (custom training, model upload, endpoint create and deploy). They handle job submission, polling, error handling and artifact passing, and they record lineage. Write custom components only for logic Google's do not cover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0142","card":"q-gcp-ml-024-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"What does <code>CustomTrainingJobOp</code> do, and what is <code>worker_pool_specs</code>?","a":"<p>It launches a Vertex AI custom training job as one pipeline step. <code>worker_pool_specs</code> describes the compute: each pool has a machine spec (machine type, accelerators), a replica count and what to run (a container or a Python package with <code>executor_image_uri</code> and <code>python_module</code>). The first pool is the chief, and extra pools can be workers or parameter servers for distributed training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0143","card":"q-gcp-ml-024-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"In a <code>worker_pool_specs</code> entry that uses <code>python_package_spec</code>, what are <code>executor_image_uri</code>, <code>package_uris</code> and <code>python_module</code>?","a":"<p><code>executor_image_uri</code> is the pre-built training container that provides the framework runtime. <code>package_uris</code> lists Cloud Storage paths of your source distribution (a tarball of your trainer code). <code>python_module</code> is the module inside it that Vertex AI starts, as <code>python -m</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0144","card":"q-gcp-ml-024-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"How does a pipeline pass the trained model from the upload step to deployment?","a":"<p>By wiring outputs to inputs: <code>ModelDeployOp(model=model_op.outputs[\"model\"], endpoint=endpoint_op.outputs[\"endpoint\"], ...)</code> takes the artifacts produced by <code>ModelUploadOp</code> and <code>EndpointCreateOp</code>. KFP infers that deploy depends on both, so it waits for them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0145","card":"q-gcp-ml-024-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"Why does a pipeline need a staging bucket and a service account?","a":"<p>The staging bucket holds trainer packages, artifacts and intermediate files. The service account is the identity under which the jobs run, and it needs permissions on that bucket and on Vertex AI. Missing permissions are the usual reason a pipeline step fails right after starting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0146","card":"q-gcp-ml-024-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"Why generate a bucket name with a random suffix, as in <code>uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]</code>?","a":"<p>Cloud Storage bucket names are globally unique across all customers, so a random suffix avoids name collisions. The trade-off is that each run makes a new bucket, so clean up or reuse buckets deliberately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0147","card":"q-gcp-ml-025-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"A pipeline run is submitted. Where do you look in Vertex AI to see which step failed and why?","a":"<p>Open the run in Vertex AI Pipelines to see the DAG, where completed steps are green and failed ones are red. Click the failing node to see its logs and error, which link to Cloud Logging for the underlying job. Per-step durations also help you size resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0148","card":"q-gcp-ml-025-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"After a pipeline that trains, uploads and deploys finishes, how do you verify the result?","a":"<p>Check that the run succeeded, that a new model version appears in Model Registry, and that the endpoint lists the deployed model and returns a test prediction. Also compare the step durations and metrics against previous runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0149","card":"q-gcp-ml-025-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"How do you clean up the resources a pipeline that deploys a model leaves behind?","a":"<p>Undeploy the model from the endpoint, then delete the endpoint (<code>gcloud ai endpoints undeploy-model</code> then <code>gcloud ai endpoints delete</code>). Delete test models and buckets too. Deployed endpoints bill for node hours until undeployed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0150","card":"q-gcp-ml-025-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"What are best practices for keeping a custom training pipeline reproducible and maintainable?","a":"<p>Start from pre-built components and add custom ones only where needed, store pipeline definitions and trainer code in source control, pin container image and library versions, parameterize project, region and bucket, and keep artifacts in Cloud Storage. Add an evaluation step as a gate before registering or deploying.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-ml-f0151","card":"q-gcp-ml-025-challenge-solution-custom-training-pipel","sec":"gcp-ml-s024","q":"A Vertex AI Pipelines run is slow and costly. How do you reduce time and cost on later runs?","a":"<p>Enable caching so unchanged steps are skipped, use the per-step timings to find the slowest stage, right-size machines (or use smaller ones for light steps), and avoid deploying to an endpoint on every run. Deploy only when the evaluation gate passes.</p>","hand":true}];
